# smartc (SmartCommit)

`smartc` is a global CLI already on PATH. It reads the current repo's git diff, AI-generates a conventional commit message, then commits. With `--auto` it accepts the message without prompts and may also push. You already know this.

Do not look it up again: no `smartc --help`, no README, no source, no web/repo search, no "what is smartc?".

## When the user asks to run smartc

Use the `smartc` CLI. Do not write the commit message with `git commit`.

Always pass `--auto` (non-interactive) and `--additional` with one short sentence: what changed and why.

```powershell
smartc --auto --additional "Add POST /roles/refresh and Roles page button"
```

- Quote `--additional`. On PowerShell, use double quotes.
- Do not omit `--additional`. Do not paste a long changelog or file list.
- Add other flags only if the user asked (`--grouped`, `--only`, `--files`, `--model`).
- Do not run `git push` yourself unless the user asked. `smartc --auto` may push on its own.

```powershell
# BAD — no instruction, hangs on confirmation
smartc

# BAD — vague or missing reason
smartc --auto --additional "update"

# GOOD
smartc --auto --additional "Print generated commit details when using --auto"
```

## After smartc finishes

The CLI output is the only source of truth (summary, committed, pushed). Report from that output and stop.

Never run git after `smartc` to verify or inspect. That includes `git status`, `git log`, `git diff`, `git show`, and `git rev-parse`. Do not re-read this rule or the repo to confirm what `smartc` is or did.
